INT. FIELD HOSPITAL TENT - FLOODED RIVER DELTA - NIGHT

Rain hammers canvas. Water laps at the plank floor. One operating lamp swings on a chain, throwing quick shadows across a table where a patient lies under a blue drape. Generators cough somewhere outside.

DR. LIESEL VANTONGEREN, 40s, mask down around her neck, gloves slick, leans over the table, hands deep in the dark work of an abdomen. Sweat runs down her temple. She does not wipe it.

A satellite phone buzzes on a crate by her elbow. She ignores it. It buzzes again. She jerks her head at the speaker button with her elbow and it clicks on.

TOMAS (V.O.)
(distorted, far away)
Liesel? Can you hear me?

LIESEL
Not now, Tomas.

TOMAS (V.O.)
It's the bridge. They've closed the bridge.

LIESEL
I'm in the middle of a resection.

TOMAS (V.O.)
The helicopter can't land. The water's too high.

She stops. Her hands go still inside the wound. The lamp swings.

LIESEL
Say again.

TOMAS (V.O.)
No extraction. Not tonight. Maybe not tomorrow.

She breathes in, slow, through her nose. Reaches for a clamp without looking, finds it, clamps. The bleeding slows. She straightens her back, once, for a second only, and the table creaks.

LIESEL
Then you tell the captain I'm keeping him here.

TOMAS (V.O.)
He needs a surgeon, Liesel. A real theater.

LIESEL
He has a surgeon.

TOMAS (V.O.)
You have a spoon and a flashlight.

LIESEL
And twenty years.

A beat of static. The rain thunders. On the table the patient shudders, one hand twitching under the drape.

TOMAS (V.O.)
(quieter)
All right. What do you need?

LIESEL
Sutures. Two units of O negative. And you, on the radio, until sunrise.

TOMAS (V.O.)
I'm here.

She sets the clamp. Reaches for a needle. Threads it with fingers that, now, finally, are shaking, and does not stop.

LIESEL
Tomas.

TOMAS (V.O.)
Yes.

LIESEL
Talk to me about anything.

TOMAS (V.O.)
Anything?

LIESEL
Anything but this.

TOMAS (V.O.)
(a small laugh)
I bought a goat yesterday.

LIESEL
You didn't.

TOMAS (V.O.)
Her name is Margaret. She's already eaten my boots.

Liesel almost smiles. The lamp swings. The needle dips and rises, dips and rises, a small steady rhythm under the downpour. The patient breathes.

She does not look up. She does not stop. She says, very quietly:

LIESEL
Tell me about Margaret.
